"National Vital Statistics Reports Volume 75, Number 2 June 9, 2026 (https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/nvsr/nvsr75/nvsr75-2.pdf)\nciteturn76view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn73search13\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4835\nL45: Among race and Hispanic-origin groups, the number of births declined 4% for Black, 2% for American Indian and Alaska Native, and less than 1% for White women from 2023 to 2024, and rose 4% for Hispanic and 5% for Asian women (Tables A, 1, 3, and 5). The change in the number of births for Native Hawaiian and Other Pacific Islander women was not significant.\nL47: #### Fertility rate\nL48: \nL49: The general fertility rate (GFR) for the United States in 2024 was a record low, 53.8 births per 1,000 women ages 15–44, down 1% from 2023 (54.5) (Table 1). The GFR has declined 21% since 2008 (13).\nL50: Among race and Hispanic-origin groups, the GFR declined less than 1% for White (51.5), 1% for Hispanic (64.3), 3% for both American Indian and Alaska Native (46.8) and Asian (46.3), and 5% for Black (50.9) women from 2023 to 2024 (Tables A and 1). The rate for Native Hawaiian and Other Pacific Islander women was essentially unchanged (72.4).\nL51: #### Age of mother\nL52: \nL53: Birth rates declined for females ages 15–34, rose for those ages 40–44, and were unchanged for those ages 10–14, 35–39, and 45–49 from 2023 to 2024.\nL54: **Teenagers**—The birth rate for those ages 15–19 in the United States in 2024 was a record low, 12.6 births per 1,000, down 4% from 2023 (13.1) (Table 2, Figure 2) (7,13,17). Since 2009, the teen birth rate has fallen to a new low each year. The teen birth rate has declined 70% since 2007 (41.5), the most recent high, and 80% since the 1991 high (61.8). The number\nL55: \nL56: National Vital Statistics Reports, Vol. 75, No. 2, June 9, 2026 3\nL57: \nL58: Figure 1. Live births and general fertility rate: United States, 1970–2024\nL291: <sup>2</sup>Includes births to race and origin groups not shown separately, such as Hispanic single-race Black, Hispanic single-race White, and non-Hispanic multiple-race women, as well as births with origin not stated.\nL292: <sup>3</sup>Includes all people of Hispanic origin of any race; see Technical Notes.\nL293: <sup>4</sup>Based on live-birth order.\nL294: <sup>5</sup>Male births per 1,000 female births.\nL295: SOURCE: National Center for Health Statistics, National Vital Statistics System, natality date file.\nL296: of births to teenagers ages 15–19 was 137,273 in 2024, down 3% from 2023 (140,977) (Table 3) (7).\nL297: The 2024 birth rate for teenagers ages 15–17 was a record low, 5.3 births per 1,000 females, down 4% from 2023 (Table 2). The rate for those ages 18–19 was also a record low, 23.6 births, down 4% from 2023. Rates for these age groups have fallen 76% and 67%, respectively, since 2007, and by 86% and 75% since 1991 (13). The birth rate for females ages 10–14 was unchanged in 2024 at 0.2 births per 1,000 females.\nL298: Among race and Hispanic-origin groups, birth rates for teenagers ages 15–19 declined for most groups: down 11% for Asian, 8% for Black, 4% for Hispanic, and 1% for White females from 2023 to 2024. The change in rates for American Indian and Alaska Native and Native Hawaiian and Other Pacific Islander females was not significant.\nL299: Women in their 20s—The birth rate for women ages 20–24 was a record low, 55.8 births per 1,000 women in 2024, down 3% from 2023 (57.7) (Table 2) (13,17). The rate for women in this age group has generally declined since 2007.\nL300: The number of births to women in their early 20s declined 1% from 2023 (Table 3) (7). The rate for women ages 25–29 was a record low, 89.5 births per 1,000 women, down 2% from 2023 (91.0). The rate for women in this age group has generally declined since 2008 (13). The number of births to women ages 25–29 rose less than 1% from 2023 to 2024 (7).\nL301: Women in their 30s—The birth rate for women ages 30–34 was 93.7 births per 1,000 women in 2024, down 1% from 2023 (94.3); the rate has declined 7% since 2017 (Table 2). The rate for ages 30–34 has been higher than the rate for ages 25–29 since 2016 (13,17). The number of births to women ages 30–34 rose by 1% from 2023 to 2024 (Table 3) (7). The birth rate for women ages 35–39 was 54.3 births per 1,000 women in 2024, unchanged from 2023 (Table 2).\nL302: The number of births to women ages 35–39 rose 3% from 2023 to 2024 (7).\nL303: Women in their 40s—The birth rate for women ages 40–44 was 12.7 births per 1,000 women in 2024, up 2% from 2023 (12.5) and the highest rate since 1965 (Table 2). The\nL304: \nL305: National Vital Statistics Reports, Vol. 75, No. 2, June 9, 2026 5\nL306: \nL307: Table B. Selected demographic characteristics of births, by Hispanic origin of mother: United States, 2024\nL308: [Mean age at first birth is the arithmetic average of the age of mothers at time of birth, computed directly from the frequency of first births by age of mother]\nL309: <table id=\"e1\">\nL310: <tr>\nL311: <th>Characteristic</th>\nL312: <th>Total<sup>1</sup></th>\nL313: <th>Central and South American</th>\nL314: <th>Cuban</th>\nL315: <th>Dominican</th>\nL316: <th>Mexican</th>\nL317: <th>Puerto Rican</th>\nL318: <th>Other and unknown Hispanic</th>\nL319: </tr>\nL320: <tr>\nL321: <td>Births</td>\nL322: <td>984,092</td>\nL323: <td>231,723</td>\nL324: <td>32,315</td>\nL325: <td>34,221</td>\nL326: <td>521,149</td>\nL327: <td>65,515</td>\nL328: <td>99,169</td>\nL329: </tr>\nL330: <tr>\nL331: <td>Sex ratio<sup>2</sup></td>\nL332: <td>1,037</td>\nL333: <td>1,033</td>\nL334: <td>1,044</td>\nL335: <td>1,032</td>\nL336: <td>1,039</td>\nL337: <td>1,039</td>\nL338: <td>1,037</td>\nL339: </tr>\nL340: <tr>\nL341: <td>Births to mothers younger than age 20</td>\nL342: <td>5.9</td>\nL343: <td>6.5</td>\nL344: <td>2.1</td>\nL345: <td>3.3</td>\nL346: <td>6.1</td>\nL347: <td>5.6</td>\nL348: <td>6.0</td>\nL349: </tr>\nL350: <tr>\nL351: <td>Births to mothers age 40 and older</td>\nL352: <td>3.7</td>\nL353: <td>4.5</td>\nL354: <td>4.0</td>\nL355: <td>4.7</td>\nL356: <td>3.4</td>\nL357: <td>3.1</td>\nL358: <td>3.9</td>\nL359: </tr>\nL360: <tr>\nL361: <td>4th and higher-order births<sup>3</sup></td>\nL362: <td>14.0</td>\nL363: <td>12.8</td>\nL364: <td>4.6</td>\nL365: <td>9.2</td>\nL366: <td>15.7</td>\nL367: <td>13.6</td>\nL368: <td>13.4</td>\nL369: </tr>\nL370: <tr>\nL371: <td>Mothers born in the 50 states or District of Columbia</td>\nL372: <td>52.3</td>\nL373: <td>15.3</td>\nL374: <td>32.9</td>\nL375: <td>30.5</td>\nL376: <td>65.2</td>\nL377: <td>73.1</td>\nL378: <td>70.8</td>\nL379: </tr>\nL380: <tr>\nL381: <td>Educational attainment of mother:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;High school diploma or higher</td>\nL382: <td>77.8</td>\nL383: <td>62.0</td>\nL384: <td>89.5</td>\nL385: <td>87.4</td>\nL386: <td>80.8</td>\nL387: <td>88.5</td>\nL388: <td>84.1</td>\nL389: </tr>\nL390: <tr>\nL391: <td>&nbsp;&nbsp;Bachelor's degree or higher</td>\nL392: <td>18.4</td>\nL393: <td>18.3</td>\nL394: <td>33.2</td>\nL395: <td>25.2</td>\nL396: <td>16.2</td>\nL397: <td>21.4</td>\nL412: Footnotes and notes below the table: 1 Includes origin not stated. 2 Male births per 1,000 female births. 3 Based on live-birth order. NOTES: People of Hispanic origin may be of any race. In this table, Hispanic women are classified only by place of origin; non-Hispanic women are not shown. Other and unknown Hispanic includes origins not otherwise classified, including Spaniard. See Technical Notes in this report.\nL415: rate for this group has generally risen since 1985 (13,17). The number of births to women in their early 40s rose 4% from 2023 to 2024 (Table 3) (7). The birth rate for women ages 45–49 (includes births to women age 50 and older) was 1.1 in 2024, unchanged from 2023 and 2022. The number of births to women age 45 and older was essentially unchanged from 2023 to 2024 (7).\nL416: Women age 50 and older—The birth rate for women ages 50–54 was 1.2 births per 10,000 women in 2024, unchanged from 2023 and 2022. Because of the small number of births to women in this age group, the birth rate for women ages 50–54 is expressed per 10,000 women. For rates shown elsewhere in this report, births to women age 50 and older are included with births to women ages 45–49 when computing birth rates by age of mother (the denominator for the rate is women ages 45–49).\nL417: A total of 1,188 births were to women age 50 and older in 2024, essentially unchanged from 1,217 in 2023 (Table 3) (7). The number of births to women in this age group has generally increased since 1997, when 144 births were reported (7).\nL418: Live-birth order\nL419: The first-birth rate for the United States was 21.3 births per 1,000 females ages 15–44 in 2024, down less than 1% from 2023 (21.4) (7) (see Table 3 for the number of births; see also Tables 5 [number of births] and 6 [birth rates] in the 2024 User Guide [8]).\nL420: First-birth rates declined for females in their teens (4% for ages 15–19), early 20s (2% for ages 20–24), and late 20s (1% for ages 25–29), and rose for women in their early 30s (1% for ages 30–34), late 30s (2% for ages 35–39) and early 40s (4% for ages 40–44).\nL421: Mean age of mother\nL422: The mean age of mothers at first birth was a record high in 2024, 27.6 years, an increase from 27.5 in 2023 (Tables A, B, and 4) (13,17). Mean age at first birth is the arithmetic average of the age of mothers at the time of birth and is computed directly from the frequency of first births by age of mother.\nL423: The increase in the mean age in 2024 reflects the decline in the proportion of first births to females younger than 20 and the concurrent rise in the proportion of first births to women age 20 and older (7).\nL424: Mean age at first birth increased for four race and Hispanic-origin groups in 2024, rising to 25.8 for Hispanic, 26.1 for Black, 28.4 for White, and 31.7 for Asian women (Table 4). The average age at first birth for American Indian and Alaska Native (24.3) and Native Hawaiian and Other Pacific Islander (25.3) women was unchanged from 2023. Among the specified Hispanic groups, average age increased to 25.4 for Mexican and 26.1 for Central and South American women (Table B) (7).\nL425: The age at first birth for Puerto Rican (26.0), Dominican (26.9), and Cuban (28.1) women in 2024 was essentially unchanged from 2023.\nL426: Total fertility rate\nL427: \nL428: The total fertility rate (TFR) for the United States in 2024 was 1,599.5 births per 1,000 women, down 1% from 2023 (1,621.0) and a record low (Table 2). TFR has generally declined since 2008, down 23%. TFR estimates the number of births that a hypothetical group of 1,000 women would have over their lifetimes, based on age-specific birth rates in a given year. Because it is calculated from age-specific birth rates, TFR is\nL429: 6  National Vital Statistics Reports, Vol. 75, No. 2, June 9, 2026\nL430: age adjusted and can be compared for populations across time, population groups, and geographic areas.\nL431: Among race and Hispanic-origin groups, the TFR declined less than 1% for White, 1% for Hispanic, 2% for American Indian and Alaska Native, 4% for Asian, and 5% for Black women from 2023 to 2024. The rate for Native Hawaiian and Other Pacific Islander women was essentially unchanged.\nL432: The TFR for the nation in 2024 remained below replacement, the level at which a given generation can exactly replace itself (generally considered to be 2,100 births per 1,000 women). The U.S. TFR has generally been below replacement since 1971 and has consistently been below replacement since 2008 (13,17). The TFR was below replacement for all race and Hispanic-origin groups in 2024 except for Native Hawaiian and Other Pacific Islander women (2,190.0 births) (Table 2).\nL433: ## Births and birth rates by state\nL434: GFR, the number of births per 1,000 females ages 15–44, declined in 19 states and D.C. from 2023 to 2024, with decreases of 1%–2% reported in 14 states (Alabama, Arizona, California, Illinois, Kansas, Maryland, Massachusetts, Michigan, New York, Ohio, Oklahoma, Pennsylvania, Texas, and Wisconsin), 3%–4% in 5 states (Connecticut, Florida, Louisiana, Mississippi, and New Jersey), and 7% in D.C. GFRs rose in two states (Colorado and West Virginia) and were essentially unchanged in the remaining 29 states (7).\nL435: Rates among the states ranged from 39.8 births per 1,000 females ages 15–44 in D.C. to 66.7 in South Dakota. (See Tables 5 and 6 for number of births and birth rates; see also Table 3 in the 2024 User Guide for births to Hispanic subgroups [8]). Among U.S. territories, the GFR declined in Puerto Rico and was essentially unchanged in Guam, Northern Marianas, and U.S. Virgin Islands.\nL437: The birth rate for unmarried women was 35.5 births per 1,000 unmarried females ages 15–44 in 2024, down 2% from 2023 (36.4) (Table 7). The 2024 nonmarital birth rate was 31% lower than the peak of 51.8 in 2007 and 2008 (13). (See Technical Notes for a description of the adjustments to calculations of rates by marital status beginning with 2017 data.)\nL438: The birth rate for married women declined by less than 1% in 2024 to 81.3 per 1,000 married females ages 15–44 from 81.6 in 2023. The marital birth rate declined 4% over 2005–2010 but rose 6% from 2010 to 2016 before declining 9% through 2024 (13).\nL439: The percentage of all births to unmarried women was 39.5% in 2024, down 1% from 2023 (40.0%) (see Tables 7 and 8 in the 2024 User Guide [8]) (7). The percentage of all births to unmarried women peaked in 2009 at 41.0% (7,13).\nL440: The percentage of nonmarital births in 2024 decreased from 2023 for Asian (11.5%), Black (68.6%), and White (26.2%) women; no significant change was seen for American Indian and Alaska Native (68.6%), Native Hawaiian and Other Pacific Islander (53.7%), and Hispanic (54.2%) women. The number of nonmarital births declined by less than 1% from 2023 (1,440,031) to 2024 (1,433,976) (7).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBiden-Harris Administration's National Security Strategy.pdf (https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Biden-Harris-Administrations-National-Security-Strategy-10.2022.pdf)\nciteturn76view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 48; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn71view0\",\"pattern\":\"Russia poses\"}); Total lines: 1800\nL228@P7: Russia and the PRC pose different challenges. Russia poses an immediate threat to the free and\nL229@P7: open international system, recklessly flouting the basic laws of the international order today, as\nL230@P7: its brutal war of aggression against Ukraine has shown. The PRC, by contrast, is the only\nL231@P7: competitor with both the intent to reshape the international order and, increasingly, the economic,\nL232@P7: diplomatic, military, and technological power to advance that objective.\nL363@P10: our interests in the others.\nL364@P10: Third, this strategy recognizes that the PRC presents America’s most consequential geopolitical\nL365@P10: challenge. Although the Indo-Pacific is where its outcomes will be most acutely shaped, there are\nL366@P10: significant global dimensions to this challenge. Russia poses an immediate and ongoing threat to\nL367@P10: the regional security order in Europe and it is a source of disruption and instability globally but it\nL368@P10: lacks the across the spectrum capabilities of the PRC. We also recognize that other smaller\nL369@P10: autocratic powers are also acting in aggressive and destabilizing ways. Most notably, Iran\nL370@P10: interferes in the internal affairs of neighbors, proliferates missiles and drones through proxies, is\nL371@P10-11: plotting to harm Americans, including former officials, and is advancing a nuclear program      \nL372@P11: 12 NATIONAL SECURITY STRATEGY\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBiden-Harris Administration's National Security Strategy.pdf (https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Biden-Harris-Administrations-National-Security-Strategy-10.2022.pdf)\nciteturn76view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 48; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn71view0\",\"pattern\":\"our democracy\"}); Total lines: 1800\nL57@P2: because we are strong at home. Our economy is dynamic. Our people are resilient and creative.\nL58@P2: Our military remains unmatched—and we will keep it that way. And it is our democracy that\nL59@P2: enables us to continually reimagine ourselves and renew our strength.\nL60@P2: So, the United States will continue to defend democracy around the world, even as we continue\nL61@P2: to do the work at home to better live up to the idea of America enshrined in our founding\nL83@P3: Implementing a Modern Industrial and Innovation Strategy................................................. 14\nL84@P3: Investing In Our People......................................................................................................... 15\nL85@P3: Strengthening Our Democracy .............................................................................................. 16\nL86@P3: Using Diplomacy to Build the Strongest Possible Coalitions................................................... 16\nL186@P6: openly and humbly with our divisions and we will work through our politics transparently and\nL187@P6: democratically. We know that for all of the effort that it takes, our democracy is worth it. It is the\nL188@P6: only way to ensure that people are truly able to live lives of dignity and freedom. This American\nL189@P6: project will never be complete—democracy is always a work in progress—but that will not stop\nL190@P6: us from defending our values and continuing to pursue our national security interests in the\nL191@P6: world. The quality of our democracy at home affects the strength and credibility of our\nL192@P6: leadership abroad—just as the character of the world we inhabit affects our ability to enjoy\nL193@P6-7: security, prosperity, and freedom at home.      \nL194@P7: 8 NATIONAL SECURITY STRATEGY\nL195@P7: Our rivals’ challenges are profound and mounting. Their problems, at both home and abroad, are\nL196@P7: associated with the pathologies inherent in highly personalized autocracies and are less easily\nL197@P7: remedied than ours. Conversely, the United States has a tradition of transforming both domestic\nL198@P7: and foreign challenges into opportunities to spur reform and rejuvenation at home. This is one\nL199@P7: reason that prophecies of American decline have repeatedly been disproven in the past—and\nL200@P7: why it has never been a good bet to bet against America. We have always succeeded when we\nL201@P7: embrace an affirmative vision for the world that addresses shared challenges and combine it with\nL202@P7: the dynamism of our democracy and the determination to out-compete our rivals.\nL203@P7: The Nature of the Competition Between Democracies and Autocracies\nL204@P7: The range of nations that supports our vision of a free, open, prosperous, and secure world is\nL205@P7: broad and powerful. It includes our democratic allies in Europe and the Indo-Pacific as well as\nL206@P7: key democratic partners around the world that share much of our vision for regional and\nL207@P7: international order even if they do not agree with us on all issues, and countries that do not\nL208@P7: embrace democratic institutions but nevertheless depend upon and support a rules-based\nL209@P7: international system.\nL210@P7: Americans will support universal human rights and stand in solidarity with those beyond our\nL211@P7: shores who seek freedom and dignity, just as we continue the critical work of ensuring equity\nL212@P7: and equal treatment under law at home. We will work to strengthen democracy around the world\nL501@P14-15: to ensure our immigration and refugee systems are fair, orderly, humane, easier to navigate, and      \nL502@P15: 16 NATIONAL SECURITY STRATEGY\nL503@P15: consistent with our values and the law. And we will take further measures to ensure the\nL504@P15: United States remains the world’s top destination for talent.\nL505@P15: Strengthening Our Democracy\nL506@P15: Our democracy is at the core of who we are, and America’s democratic experiment has long\nL507@P15: been a source of inspiration for people around the world. Our system of government enshrines\nL508@P15: the rule of law and strives to protect the equality and dignity of all individuals. Deliberation and\nL509@P15: informed debate propel us to correct our mistakes, better meet public needs, and expand the\nL510@P15: circle of opportunity. We have not always lived up to our ideals and in recent years our\nL511@P15: democracy has been challenged from within. But we have never walked away from our ideals\nL512@P15: and in each challenging moment, citizens have stepped forward to uphold them. In times of crisis\nL513@P15: or lapses in judgment, we look to more democracy—not less—to forge the path forward. Our\nL514@P15: democracy is a work in progress—and by reckoning with and remedying our own shortcomings,\nL515@P15: we can inspire others around the world to do the same.\nL516@P15: As Americans, we must all agree that the people’s verdict, as expressed in elections, must be\nL517@P15: respected and protected. We also believe that critical reforms continue to be needed to strengthen\nL518@P15: our system of governance. This is why we have taken executive action and urged essential\nL519@P15: legislation to protect and promote voting rights and expand democratic participation, and why we\nL520@P15: are building on the work of generations of activists to advance equity and root out systemic\nL521@P15: disparities in our laws, policies, and institutions. Indeed, pluralism, inclusion, and diversity are a\nL522@P15: source of national strength in a rapidly changing world. We are reaffirming the rights to free\nL523@P15: speech, a free press, peaceful assembly, and other core civil liberties. And at the same time, we\nL524@P15: are standing up to threats to our democracy such as domestic terrorism by implementing our\nL525@P15: nation’s first-ever National Strategy for Countering Domestic Terrorism and tackling head-on\nL526@P15: global forces like weaponized corruption, information manipulation operations, political\nL527@P15: interference, and attacks on the rule of law, including in elections. America will not tolerate\nL528@P15: foreign interference in our elections. We will act decisively to defend, and deter disruptions to\nL529@P15: our democratic processes, and we will respond to future interference using all appropriate tools\nL530@P15: of national power.\nL531@P15: Using Diplomacy to Build the Strongest Possible Coalitions\nL532@P15: The United States’ unrivaled network of allies and partners protects and advances our interests\nL533@P15: around the world—and is the envy of our adversaries. Building on this network, we will\nL534@P15: assemble the strongest possible coalitions to advance and defend a world that is free, open,\nL535@P15: prosperous, and secure. These coalitions will include all nations that share these objectives. At\nL536@P15: the heart of this coalition, to ensure it is as transformative as possible, are democratic nations\nL537@P15: who share our interests and values. To make our coalitions as inclusive as possible, we will also\nL538@P15: work with any country that supports a rules-based order while we continue to press all partners to\nL539@P15: respect and advance democracy and human rights.\nL540@P15: Transformative Cooperation\nL541@P15: To solve the toughest problems the world faces, we need to produce dramatically greater levels\nL542@P15: of cooperation. The key to doing this is to recognize that the core of our inclusive coalition are\nL543@P15-16: those partners who most closely share our interests. America’s treaty alliances with other      \nL544@P16: NATIONAL SECURITY STRATEGY 17\nL545@P16: democratic countries are foundational to our strategy and central to almost everything we do to\nL546@P16: make the world more peaceful and prosperous. Our NATO and bilateral treaty allies should\nL547@P16: never doubt our will and capacity to stand with them against aggression and intimidation. As we\nL548@P16: modernize our military and work to strengthen our democracy at home, we will call on our allies\nL549@P16: to do the same, including by investing in the type of capabilities and undertaking the planning\nL550@P16: necessary to bolster deterrence in an increasingly confrontational world.\nL551@P16: America’s alliances and partnerships have played a critical role in our national security policy for\nL552@P16: eight decades, and must be deepened and modernized to do so into the future. NATO has\nL553@P16: responded with unity and strength to deter further Russian aggression in Europe, even as NATO\nL554@P16: also adopted a broad new agenda at the 2022 Madrid Summit to address systemic challenges\nL555@P16: from the PRC and other security risks from cyber to climate, as well as agreeing to Finland and\nL828@P23: United States and the PRC to coexist peacefully, and share in and contribute to human progress\nL829@P23: together.\nL830@P23: Our strategy toward the PRC is threefold: 1) to invest in the foundations of our strength at home –\nL831@P23: our competitiveness, our innovation, our resilience, our democracy, 2) to align our efforts with our\nL832@P23: network of allies and partners, acting with common purpose and in common cause, and 3) compete\nL941@P25: prevent Russia from causing further harm to European security, democracy, and institutions.\nL942@P25: Third, the United States will deter and, as necessary, respond to Russian actions that threaten\nL943@P25: core U.S. interests, including Russian attacks on our infrastructure and our democracy. Fourth,\nL944@P25: Russia’s conventional military will have been weakened, which will likely increase Moscow’s\nL945@P25: reliance on nuclear weapons in its military planning. The United States will not allow Russia, or\nL1779@P47: will be evaluated by an overriding metric: whether it makes life better, safer, and fairer for the\nL1780@P47: people of the United States, and whether it lifts up the countries and people around the world\nL1781@P47: who share our vision for the future.\nL1782@P47: We are motivated by a clear vision of what success looks like at the end of this decisive decade.\nL1783@P47: By enhancing our industrial capacity, investing in our people, and strengthening our democracy,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBiden-Harris Administration's National Security Strategy.pdf (https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Biden-Harris-Administrations-National-Security-Strategy-10.2022.pdf)\nciteturn76view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 48; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn71view0\",\"pattern\":\"only competitor\"}); Total lines: 1800\nL228@P7: Russia and the PRC pose different challenges. Russia poses an immediate threat to the free and\nL229@P7: open international system, recklessly flouting the basic laws of the international order today, as\nL230@P7: its brutal war of aggression against Ukraine has shown. The PRC, by contrast, is the only\nL231@P7: competitor with both the intent to reshape the international order and, increasingly, the economic,\nL232@P7: diplomatic, military, and technological power to advance that objective.\nL233@P7: Just as the United States and countries around the world benefited greatly from the post-Cold\nL234@P7: War international order, so too did the PRC and Russia. The PRC’s economy and geopolitical\nL235@P7: influence grew rapidly. Russia joined the G8 and G20 and recovered economically in the 2000s.\nL236@P7: And yet, they concluded that the success of a free and open rules-based international order posed\nL811@P22: PRC while constraining a still profoundly dangerous Russia.\nL812@P22: China\nL813@P22: The PRC is the only competitor with both the intent to reshape the international order and,\nL814@P22: increasingly, the economic, diplomatic, military, and technological power to do it. Beijing has\nL815@P22: ambitions to create an enhanced sphere of influence in the Indo-Pacific and to become the\nL816@P22: world’s leading power. It is using its technological capacity and increasing influence over\nL817@P22: international institutions to create more permissive conditions for its own authoritarian model,\nL818@P22: and to mold global technology use and norms to privilege its interests and values. Beijing\nL819@P22: frequently uses its economic power to coerce countries. It benefits from the openness of the\nL820@P22: international economy while limiting access to its domestic market, and it seeks to make the\nL821@P22-23: world more dependent on the PRC while reducing its own dependence on the world. The PRC is      \n"
